{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Root-cause evidence (2026-11-18 PST, rebar-session-3d705fec), on the no-remote clone of origin/tickets b878d77e28d (44,311 SNAPSHOT ledger entries). Every entry was checked offline with rebar's own `registry.verify` against identity 594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d's full keyring (all null-signer entries plus a ~2% sample of the rest). UUID and content-hash subject binding holds on all 44,311.\n\n**Class A: 4,987 entries are signed with the MCP box's op-cert environment keys, not with an authorship key.**\n- 61689bf468f5: 2,430 entries, 2026-08-26 09:51 PDT to 2026-09-07 15:36 PDT.\n- 6e2a8414bf53: 2,557 entries, 2026-09-15 10:59 PDT to 2026-11-18 11:55 PST, so new writes are still being signed this way.\n- Both keys are in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` under op-cert env 9f1c8e42-7a3b-4d5e-b6c1-2f0a9d8e7c65. The second was added in 665d1d04801, \"Register rotated op-cert trusted environment key\", on 2026-09-07.\n- Neither key is on identity 594c's keyring. All 4,987 fail cryptographic verification against it (`mismatch`).\n- Mechanism: in `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`, the `mcp` service sets `REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY: /run/secrets/opcert-ed25519-key`, and `_apply_authorship` (`src/rebar/_commands/_seam.py`) signs every MCP-written event with that key under principal 594c. The `review-bot` service uses `rebar-bot-signing-key` instead. A live event is affected too: jira-reb-529/...0ecafe53...-COMMENT.json is signed by 61689bf468f5. These entries are genuinely unverifiable against 594c's in-band keys.\n\n**Class B: 1,320 entries are signed with keys that ARE on 594c's keyring (3d4e271c8a89: 1,301; a060aace07c3: 19), but compaction recorded `signer_pubkey: null`.**\n- All 1,320 verify (`certified`) against the keyring today.\n- `_verify_signed` returns `bad-signature` for any ledger entry with a null signer before it tries to verify, so these are misclassified.\n- 1,294 of the 1,301 3d4e entries were compacted by env b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126.\n- This contradicts the earlier 40-entry sample (4 of 40 verified through `identify_signer`). My check builds the trust root directly from the identity's KEY_ADD/SNAPSHOT keyring records. The earlier check resolved the keyring through `show_ticket`. The method difference is not yet established.\n\n**Remaining ~700 of the 7,007 bad-signature verdicts:** not broken down. In the sample, every set-signer ledger entry verified (775 of 775). The one tail example is a live event signed by an op-cert key.\n\nOptions need an operator decision about signing and trust policy. This ticket stays open; no code or keyring change was made."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795045554969473738,"uuid":"0fefba9d-d78e-4eb7-b645-ac178087c850"}